Below is a minimal example that shows how you could wire up the queue returned by `functions.B` so that:

* The queue is created with **concurrency = 5**  
* Each task simply logs a message (`Task X`)  
* When the queue becomes saturated it prints `"Queue is saturated"`  
* When it becomes unsaturated it prints `"Queue is unsaturated"`

```js
// 1️⃣ Create the queue – note that all numeric arguments are passed as strings
const queue = functions.B({
  t: "5",          // concurrency level (string representation of 5)
  // e and n are optional – we leave them out for now
});

// 2️⃣ Attach event listeners for saturation/unsaturation
queue.on("saturated",   () => console.log("Queue is saturated"));
queue.on("unsaturated", () => console.log("Queue is unsaturated"));

// 3️⃣ Add some sample tasks – each task is a function that logs its own id
for (let i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {
  const taskId = i;
  queue.addTask(() => console.log(`Task ${taskId}`));
}
```

### What’s happening under the hood?

| Step | What happens | Why it matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| 1️⃣ | `functions.B` is called with `{ t: "5" }`. The function parses the string `"5"` into a number and creates a queue instance that will run at most 5 tasks concurrently. | Concurrency controls how many tasks can run at once. |
| 2️⃣ | `.on()` attaches callbacks to the queue’s internal event emitter. When the queue’s active‑task count reaches the concurrency limit, the `"saturated"` event fires; when it drops below that limit, `"unsaturated"` fires. | Lets you react to changes in load. |
| 3️⃣ | `.addTask()` pushes a new task onto the queue. The queue will automatically start executing tasks up to the concurrency limit. | Adds work to the system. |

> **Tip:** If you need to pause or resume the queue later, you can call `queue.pause()` or `queue.resume()` (assuming those methods exist on the returned object).

Feel free to adapt the task logic or the number of tasks to fit your real use case!

